 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Distributed Volume Ray Tracing with Global Kd-Tree](1774-distributed-volume-ray-tracing-with-global-kd-tree.md)
-A distributed rendering method for load balancing where adjacent tetrahedra are scattered across nodes rather than clustered, using a global kd-tree to restore ordering during rendering.
+A distributed rendering method for load balancing where adjacent tetrahedra are scattered across nodes rather than clustered, using a global kd-tree to restore ordering during rendering. Unlike SDVR which clusters adjacent tetrahedra into brick-shaped chunks, this method scatters them to maintain load balance for close views, at the cost of requiring a global kd-tree.
 
 ### [Distributed Shared Virtual Memory Volume Rendering](1916-distributed-shared-virtual-memory-volume-rendering.md)
-Distributed Shared Virtual Memory (DSVM) Volume Rendering is a method for rendering tera-scale volumes on GPU clusters by mapping the entire volume into a single shared 3D virtual memory space accessible by all nodes. It solves the problem of managing massive datasets that exceed the physical memory of individual GPUs by using on-demand paging of small voxel blocks, allowing multiple nodes to render disjoint parts of the volume simultaneously without explicit data partitioning or load balancing. It differs from sibling methods like Distributed Volume Ray Tracing with Global Kd-Tree by relying on a flat virtual memory address space with hardware-assisted page fault handling rather than a distributed spatial index, and by targeting small GPU clusters where implicit load balancing via interleaved distribution units suffices.
+Distributed Shared Virtual Memory (DSVM) Volume Rendering is a method for rendering tera-scale volumes on GPU clusters by mapping the entire volume into a single shared 3D virtual memory space accessible by all nodes. It solves the problem of managing massive datasets that exceed the physical memory of individual GPUs by using on-demand paging of small voxel blocks, allowing multiple nodes to render disjoint parts of the volume simultaneously without explicit data partitioning or load balancing. It differs from SDVR by relying on a flat virtual memory address space with hardware-assisted page fault handling rather than a distributed spatial index, and by targeting small GPU clusters where implicit load balancing via interleaved distribution units suffices.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Scalability:** The primary goal is scalability: rendering time remains constant as the dataset size increases, provided additional computational nodes are added [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** A prototype ran successfully at interactive rates on up to 32 nodes of a PC cluster equipped with Nvidia GeForce-3 graphics cards [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Hardware Requirements:** Runs on LINUX/UNIX systems [S1]. Uses OpenGL and MPI [S1][S2, unofficial]. Graphics hardware is optional but required for peak performance [S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Speed-up is limited by communications overhead, which causes the performance curve to level out after a certain number of threads (5 for slicing, 18 for projection) [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** Suitable for large PC clusters (16-1000+ nodes) rendering large unstructured or curvilinear grid datasets where scalability is critical [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for small clusters where communication overhead dominates, or when hardware acceleration is unavailable and peak performance is required [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Scalability:** The primary goal is scalability: as the data set size increases, if additional computational nodes are provided, rendering time remains constant [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** A prototype ran successfully at interactive rates on up to 32 nodes of a PC cluster with Nvidia GeForce-3 graphics cards [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Hardware Requirements:** Can run on any platform with or without graphics hardware, though peak performance requires hardware [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance may be limited by communication overhead, especially as the number of nodes increases [S1]. The system is designed for large PC clusters (16-1000+ nodes) [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Suitable for large unstructured datasets that cannot fit in a single node's memory, and when a cluster with MPI support is available.
+*   **When Not to Use:** For small datasets that fit in memory, or when high-performance graphics hardware is not available and software rendering is too slow.
 
 ## Background
-- **Origin:** The Scalable Distributed Volume Rendering (SDVR) System was developed for unstructured data, targeting large PC clusters [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Paper:** The method is described in "Volume Rendering for Curvilinear and Unstructured Grids" [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Institution:** Work performed under the auspices of the U.S. Department of Energy by Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Key Contributors:** Randall Frank (conversations), Mark Duchaineau (cell slicing code) [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **History:** The system was designed to address the need for scalable volume visualization on large clusters, leveraging MPI and OpenGL [S1][S2, unofficial].
